  :adr/body
  "# ADR-2607196300: Opaque one-shot PostgreSQL cancellation authority

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-19

## Decision

BackendKeyData is consumed inside the PostgreSQL provider boundary. The PID
and secret key are registered against the live affine TLS handle through the
purpose-bound `pg_cancel_register` host operation. The host returns only an
opaque i32 authority; neither scalar is copied into consumer memory.

Using the authority invokes `pg_cancel`. The host removes it before attempting
network I/O, so success and failure are both one-shot. It can connect only to
the canonical host, port and numeric address pinned by the authenticated
session. It emits only PostgreSQL's fixed 16-byte CancelRequest and cannot be
used as a socket, arbitrary payload or endpoint selector. Handle and request
quotas default to zero and require secret/write capability opt-in.

PostgreSQL CancelRequest is intentionally a separate non-TLS protocol message;
the security boundary is therefore the unguessable backend key plus exact
endpoint pinning. The backend key remains inside the native/provider TCB.

## Evidence

Released PostgreSQL 14.19 qualification opens a cancellable
SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS session, starts `select pg_sleep(10)`, cancels it from an
independent component, requires SQLSTATE `57014` and ReadyForQuery `I`, then
verifies a second use of the authority is denied and all handles are closed.

This closes cancellation. Extended query/prepared statements, pooling,
credential/certificate rotation, version/cloud matrices and long-duration
soak remain open."}]
